 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the labor law, in relation to clarifying methods for the payment of wages and authorizing the payment of wages by use of payroll cards",
    "basePrintNo": "S2590",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2590-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §192, add §192-a, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S2590\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the labor law, in relation to\nclarifying methods for the payment of wages and authorizing the\npayment of wages by use of payroll cards\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThe bill would allow New York employers to provide for the payment of\nwages on payroll cards.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection I amends the heading of section 192 of the Labor Law.\n\nSection II amends subdivision 1 of Section 192 of the Labor Law to\nspecify the methods under which wages are paid wages shall be paid in\nthe following:\n\nA. In lawful money of the United States\n\nB. By check\n\nC. By electronic automated fund transfer into an account in the same\nname of the employee at a financial institution designed by the\nemployee; provided the employee voluntarily gives written or\nelectronic authorization in advance\n\nD. By credit to a payroll card in accordance with section 192-a of\nthis article\n\nSection III amends the Labor Law by adding a new section 192-a which\nspecifies the payment of wages using payroll cards.\n\n1) Definitions\n\nA. \"Payroll Card\"\n\nB. \"Payroll Card Account\"\n\nC. \"Payroll Card Issuer\"\n\n2) If an employer pays wages to employees by credit to a payroll card:\n\nA. Employees must be allowed at least one withdrawal or transfer from\nthe payroll each and every pay period without charge for any amount up\nto and including the full amount of the employee's wages for the\nperiod, except as provided in paragraph B.\n\nB. Employees must be allowed at least one withdrawal or transfer from\nthe payroll each and every pay period without charge for any amount up\nto and including the full amount of the employee's wages for the\nperiod, if wages are paid more frequently than weekly.\n\n3) If an employer offers payroll cards to deliver wages or other\ncompensation to their employees they must also provide the option of\n\n\nreceiving wages or other compensation by electronic fund transfer in\naccordance with section 191-c of this article.\n\n4) Employees must be provided with a means of checking their payroll\ncard balance through an automated telephone system and one additional\nelectronic means without cost.\n\n5) Employees must be provided with notice of the following items in:\n\nA. Terms and conditions relating to the use of the payroll card\n\nB. Methods available to employees for accessing wages without costs.\n\nC. Methods available to employees for checking the balance in the\npayroll card account without costs; and\n\nD. A statement as to whether third parties may assess additional fees\n\n6) Requires that wages paid on a payroll card must be insured by the\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union\nAdministration, or another entity on a pass through basis to the\nemployee.\n\n7) Nothing in this section shall be construed to preempt or override\nthe terms of any collective bargaining agreement with respect to\nmethods of an employer's payment of wages, salary or other\ncompensation due to employees.\n\nSection IV - This act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it\nshall have become law\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation recognizes the benefits to employers by providing the\noption to pay and to employees by providing the option to receive\nwages on a payroll card. This option would give employees a safe and\nreliable means to receive wages, by allowing them to access their\nwages at ATMs, banks and merchant locations without having to incur\nfees for check cashing or carry large amounts of cash. This bill would\nalso provide financial institutions with a manner to deliver services\nto traditionally \"unbanked\" people. Payroll cards provide employers\nwith a less expensive way to deliver wages than paper checks, which is\nparticularly useful when an employee does not have access to direct\ndeposit services.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.6288A of 2012\nS.4392A of 2014\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nNinetieth day after it shall have become law.\n\n\n",
